dex\":83,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:32:25Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Considering Roman Dominating Functions**\\n\\nConsidering a Roman dominating function, the focus shifts to understanding what the absence of nodes with a value of 2 implies. Specifically examining what happens when $V_2$ is empty within the function's definition.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"echo \\\\\\\"Check task-82\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"1000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Wait for task-82\\\"\"}}]}\n83: {\"step_index\":84,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:32:29Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:32:29+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:32:29+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nCheck task-82\\r\\n\\n\"}\n84: {\"step_index\":85,\"source\":\"SYSTEM\",\"type\":\"SYSTEM_MESSAGE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:32:29Z\",\"content\":\"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\\n\\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-11T18:32:27Z sender=7841936c-55c7-41c5-92b8-9773cb8ee932/task-82 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=Task id \\\"7841936c-55c7-41c5-92b8-9773cb8ee932/task-82\\\" finished with result:\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nLINT: ok\\r\\nCHECK: elaborated. Allowed axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.\\r\\n       'sorryAx' in a list above means that theorem is unfinished.\\r\\n\\n\\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\"}\n85: {\"step_index\":86,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:32:29Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Examining Vertex Properties**\\n\\nConsidering the case where the set of vertices mapped to 2 is non-empty, a vertex *c* exists with *f(c) = 2*. Any vertex *v* mapped to 0 must have an adjacent vertex *u* where *f(u) = 2*.\\n\\nUsing the graph's symmetry, *u* is also adjacent to *v*. Thus, *v* belongs to a 1-ball centered at some vertex *u* in the set of vertices mapped to 2, and any *u* mapped to 2 is within its own 0-ball, and thus also its 1-ball. Consequently, every vertex *x*...\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Single Mapped Vertices**\\n\\nInvestigating the scenario where only one vertex is mapped to 2, and the set of vertices mapped to 0 is potentially non-empty. This leads to the conclusion that the ball of radius 1 centered at the vertex with weight 2 contains all other vertices. Further explorations consider the cases where the set of vertices mapped to 0 may be of size 0 or 1.\\n\\nIf the set of vertices mapped to 0 has size 0, the radius is less than or equal to 1, thus radius + 1 is less than or equal to 2. If the set of vertices mapped to 0 has size 1, radius + 1 is less than or equal to 3. The overall pattern is becoming clear.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating the General Case**\\n\\nExamining the case where the set of vertices with weight 2 has size greater than or equal to 2, it seems possible to generalize the radius + 1 bound.  The logic established earlier for single vertices with weight 2 provides the foundation to tackle this broader scenario.  If multiple vertices have weight 2, the reasoning on connectivity components would still seem to apply. Therefore, investigating this with multiple vertices with weight 2 remains valid and crucial.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating Conjecture Falsification**\\n\\nThe problem involves settling an open graph theory conjecture, and initial calculations show that roman weight is bounded, so radius+1 ≤ roman_domination_number is a possible conjecture. The \\\"open\\\" nature of the conjecture suggests exploring possible counterexamples.  Current work focuses on generating or seeking existing graph data. Attempting to determine why prior scripts failed to uncover counterexamples.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\\\\n# What is the maximum radius of a connected graph with V = N[u1] cup N[u2]?\\\\n# Vertices: u1, u2, A (neighbors of u1 only), B (neighbors of u2 only), C (common neighbors).\\\\n# For any vertex x in N[u1], dist(u1, x) <= 1.\\\\n# What is dist(u1, y) for y in B?\\\\n# Since G is connected, there must be some edge between N[u1] and N[u2].\\\\n# Case 1: edge between u1 and u2: then dist(u1, y) <= 1 + 1 = 2 for all y in B.\\\\n# Then ecc(u1) <= 2! So rad <= 2!\\\\n# Case 2: u1 not adj u2. Common neighbor c in C:\\\\n# u1 - c - u2. Then dist(u1, y) <= 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 for all y in B.\\\\n# And what about ecc(c)?\\\\n# For any x in N[u1], dist(c, x) <= dist(c, u1) + dist(u1, x) <= 1 + 1 = 2.\\\\n# For any y in N[u2], dist(c, y) <= dist(c, u2) + dist(u2, y) <= 1 + 1 = 2.\\\\n# So ecc(c) <= 2! Thus rad <= 2!\\\\n# Case 3: u1 not adj u2, C is empty (no common neighbors).\\\\n# Then any edge between N[u1] and N[u2] is between some a in A and b in B!\\\\n# Path: u1 - a - b - u2.\\\\n# What is ecc(a)?\\\\n# For any x in N[u1]: a - u1 - x, so dist(a, x) <= 2.\\\\n# For any y in N[u2]: a - b - u2 - y, so dist(a, y) <= 3.\\\\n# So ecc(a) <= 3!\\\\n# Could ecc(a) = 3 and for all other vertices ecc >= 3, so rad = 3?\\\\n# Yes! Rad can be 3!\\\\n# Can rad ever be 4 when V = N[u1] cup N[u2]?\\\\n# No! Because ecc(a) <= 3 ALWAYS!\\\\nprint(\\\\\\\"Max radius of 2-star graph is 3.\\\\\\\")\\\\n'\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Running command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Analyze 2-star graphs\\\"\"}}]}\n86: {\"step_index\":87,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-0